WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:17.212
Welcome to another episode of Superstars in History.

2
00:00:17.692 --> 00:00:19.354
I'm your host, Faye Moss.

3
00:00:20.415 --> 00:00:26.601
One of my favorite books is Little Women, so I'm super happy to welcome today's guest.

4
00:00:27.662 --> 00:00:29.724
She was quite a character!

5
00:00:30.272 --> 00:00:35.298
This fierce, ambitious girl struggled with her temper and her three sisters.

6
00:00:36.279 --> 00:00:40.404
Growing up in poverty, she was determined to become a successful writer.

7
00:00:40.424 --> 00:00:49.916
Oh, but our guest isn't the heroine of the book, Jo March—it's her creator, author Louisa May Alcott!

8
00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:05.521
Hello, Miss Moss.

9
00:01:06.962 --> 00:01:09.424
I really appreciate your coming today!

10
00:01:10.365 --> 00:01:12.447
First let's hear about you and your family.

11
00:01:13.387 --> 00:01:17.711
You were born in Pennsylvania on your father's birthday in 1832.

12
00:01:18.792 --> 00:01:20.373
What were you like as a child?

13
00:01:21.334 --> 00:01:23.035
I was the second of four girls.

14
00:01:24.096 --> 00:01:29.740
My parents, Bronson and Abigail, were surprised that I was such an active, stubborn child.

15
00:01:30.721 --> 00:01:35.417
A boy couldn't be my friend until I'd beaten him in a race, and I didn't like girls

16
00:01:35.457 --> 00:01:36.758
who refused to climb trees.

17
00:01:37.839 --> 00:01:41.381
Throughout my life I loved to run, which my father encouraged.

18
00:01:42.762 --> 00:01:46.325
That was an unusual attitude in the nineteenth century.

19
00:01:47.226 --> 00:01:50.969
I've heard you based many Little Women characters on your family.

20
00:01:51.849 --> 00:01:53.911
Was your father a minister like Mr.

21
00:01:53.931 --> 00:02:00.596
March? My father was a teacher and philosopher, who was more interested in ideas than money.

22
00:02:01.716 --> 00:02:06.357
He was always in debt and depended upon family and friends to pay our bills.

23
00:02:07.458 --> 00:02:09.658
Because we were poor, we often moved.

24
00:02:10.619 --> 00:02:16.680
My dear mother, who inspired the character of Marmee, was always frustrated by my father's lack of interest

25
00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:17.200
in money.

26
00:02:18.221 --> 00:02:23.622
She took in sewing and became one of the first social workers in the United States, working with

27
00:02:23.702 --> 00:02:24.622
poor immigrants.

28
00:02:25.803 --> 00:02:28.063
Did you and your sisters have to work too?

29
00:02:28.083 --> 00:02:29.324
Oh, yes.

30
00:02:30.568 --> 00:02:37.070
At age 15 I vowed to do anything—teach, sew, write—to help support my family.

31
00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:41.551
I did all those things and more, including working as a laundress.

32
00:02:42.691 --> 00:02:45.912
I liked sewing because I could plan my stories while I worked.

33
00:02:46.853 --> 00:02:50.914
I'd always enjoyed writing and started composing poems at age eight.

34
00:02:51.954 --> 00:02:56.075
My parents expected us to keep journals, which they often read and commented upon.

35
00:02:56.095 --> 00:02:58.736
Oh, I'd hate that!

36
00:03:00.136 --> 00:03:04.440
My first book, Flower Fables, was published when I was 22.

37
00:03:05.481 --> 00:03:06.582
It was a children's book.

38
00:03:07.583 --> 00:03:12.927
I much preferred writing gothic thrillers for adults, which I wrote under a pen name.

39
00:03:14.248 --> 00:03:18.251
These stories were often quite scandalous, but they brought in money.

40
00:03:19.593 --> 00:03:23.516
I was obsessed with writing to the point that I often went without food or sleep.

41
00:03:24.537 --> 00:03:28.180
I even taught myself to write with both hands so I could keep working.

42
00:03:29.152 --> 00:03:31.153
That's what I call determination!

43
00:03:32.033 --> 00:03:33.993
How did you come to write Little Women?

44
00:03:35.074 --> 00:03:39.295
In the late 1860s, my publisher wanted me to write a book for girls.

45
00:03:40.475 --> 00:03:46.617
Reluctantly I sat down at my semicircular desk in Concord, Massachusetts.

46
00:03:47.617 --> 00:03:52.378
My publisher wasn't enthusiastic about my first few chapters, and neither was I.

47
00:03:53.418 --> 00:03:59.812
But when the book was finished—I'd produced almost five hundred pages in less than three months—we liked it.

48
00:04:00.713 --> 00:04:02.294
And so did the girls we showed it to.

49
00:04:03.214 --> 00:04:08.417
Let's dig into the similarities—and differences—between the Alcotts and the Marches.

50
00:04:09.538 --> 00:04:16.241
Little Women is set during the American Civil War, which the Northern and Southern states fought over slavery,

51
00:04:16.301 --> 00:04:17.262
among other issues.

52
00:04:18.222 --> 00:04:19.983
When the book opens, Mr.

53
00:04:20.063 --> 00:04:24.506
March is away from home, serving as a chaplain for Northern troops.

54
00:04:25.947 --> 00:04:28.248
I guess your father fought for the North too?

55
00:04:29.632 --> 00:04:35.915
No. Both my parents were abolitionists who believed that slaves should be freed, as did I.

56
00:04:36.755 --> 00:04:39.436
They'd even hidden a runaway slave when I was a child.

57
00:04:40.476 --> 00:04:42.497
But my father never served in the army.

58
00:04:43.658 --> 00:04:45.959
I was the one who wanted to fight in the war.

59
00:04:46.919 --> 00:04:49.580
That wasn't allowed, of course, because I was a woman.

60
00:04:50.541 --> 00:04:54.122
So I signed up for three months as an army nurse in Washington DC.

61
00:04:55.162 --> 00:04:58.884
After only six weeks, I became gravely ill and was sent home.

62
00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:05.483
Unfortunately I was given a medicine containing mercury, and my health was never the same again.

63
00:05:06.504 --> 00:05:11.327
But I kept writing—as much as 14 hours a day—because my parents had so many bills.

64
00:05:12.508 --> 00:05:14.389
Tell us something about your sisters.

65
00:05:15.709 --> 00:05:20.732
My oldest sister was Anna; I modeled Meg, Jo's older sister, on her.

66
00:05:21.773 --> 00:05:26.336
Anna wanted to be an actress, and we were always putting on plays for friends.

67
00:05:27.516 --> 00:05:31.218
Like Meg, Anna worked as a governess and was the first to get married.

68
00:05:32.199 --> 00:05:35.921
In fact, Little Women originally ended with Meg's engagement.

69
00:05:36.861 --> 00:05:41.184
But I wrote a second part to the story because readers wanted more about the March girls.

70
00:05:42.104 --> 00:05:45.706
They especially wanted Jo to marry her neighbor Laurie.

71
00:05:46.827 --> 00:05:51.870
I hated this traditional plot because I didn't think women had to marry to be happy.

72
00:05:52.770 --> 00:05:54.992
That's what Jo says to Meg in the book!

73
00:05:56.172 --> 00:05:58.674
Did you have a real-life neighbor like Laurie?

74
00:06:00.475 --> 00:06:02.096
Everyone asks me that!

75
00:06:02.276 --> 00:06:05.218
No, he was based on a man I'd met in Europe.

76
00:06:06.359 --> 00:06:10.322
These days the two parts of Little Women are published as one book.

77
00:06:11.362 --> 00:06:13.784
So much happens in the second part!

78
00:06:14.665 --> 00:06:15.645
Beth dies.

79
00:06:16.046 --> 00:06:21.709
Amy goes to Europe to become an artist and—spoiler alert!—marries Laurie.

80
00:06:22.550 --> 00:06:25.332
How much of that is based on your family's history?

81
00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:33.265
My younger sister Elizabeth died before Anna married, but in the novel I postponed Beth's death until after

82
00:06:33.325 --> 00:06:33.965
Meg's wedding.

83
00:06:34.966 --> 00:06:40.430
I based the character of Amy on my youngest sister, May, who studied art in Europe and married

84
00:06:40.450 --> 00:06:41.591
a man from Switzerland.

85
00:06:42.732 --> 00:06:47.615
I was shocked when Meg's husband suddenly dies in the sequel Little Men.

86
00:06:48.856 --> 00:06:51.118
Sadly that happened in real life too.

87
00:06:52.238 --> 00:06:54.160
Anna's husband died unexpectedly.

88
00:06:55.092 --> 00:06:59.993
His death spurred me to write Little Men so that Anna and her boys would have some money.

89
00:07:01.014 --> 00:07:03.694
In that novel Jo is married and has two children.

90
00:07:04.835 --> 00:07:08.136
Instead of writing, she's running a school for boys with her husband.

91
00:07:09.276 --> 00:07:12.857
I later wrote a sequel to that book called Jo's Boys.

92
00:07:14.057 --> 00:07:15.818
What a remarkable career!

93
00:07:16.838 --> 00:07:21.920
When you were 15, you vowed to be rich, famous, and happy before you died.

94
00:07:22.340 --> 00:07:22.840
Were you?

95
00:07:24.068 --> 00:07:26.009
I enjoyed the money but not the fame.

96
00:07:27.009 --> 00:07:31.812
When fans arrived at my house, I sometimes pretended to be a servant and said Miss Alcott was

97
00:07:31.932 --> 00:07:34.573
out. Or I fled into the woods.

98
00:07:35.573 --> 00:07:39.655
But what I really wanted to do was to squirt those people with the garden hose!

99
00:07:42.957 --> 00:07:48.019
Well, I don't want to get wet, so we'll let you return to history now.

100
00:07:49.099 --> 00:07:50.160
Thank you, Miss Moss.

101
00:07:54.192 --> 00:07:58.193
Unlike Jo March, Louisa May Alcott never married.

102
00:07:59.234 --> 00:08:05.515
During her lifetime she published more than thirty books as well as hundreds of short stories, poems, and

103
00:08:05.695 --> 00:08:11.997
articles. On March 6, 1888, she died at the age of 55.

104
00:08:13.117 --> 00:08:20.420
Her most famous work remains Little Women, which has been translated into more than fifty languages and been

105
00:08:20.440 --> 00:08:24.439
adapted for film, theater, opera, and ballet.

106
00:08:25.620 --> 00:08:27.322
I hope you enjoyed today's show.

107
00:08:28.383 --> 00:08:29.684
See you again next time!
